q('An adult has fever, neck stiffness, altered consciousness and suspected bacterial meningitis. Lumbar puncture cannot be performed promptly. What is the best next step?', 'Obtain blood cultures promptly and start appropriate empirical antibiotics without waiting for LP',[
 ('Delay all antibiotics until CSF is available','Treatment delay can worsen outcome.'),('Use antipyretics alone until a rash appears','A rash is not required for bacterial meningitis.'),('Wait for a positive blood culture before treatment','Culture results are too slow for the initial emergency decision.')],
 'Diagnostic sampling must not create a harmful treatment delay', '**Bacterial meningitis is a medical emergency.**',['Perform LP before therapy when promptly feasible and safe.','If deferred, treat and obtain CSF when the safety and clinical situation permit.'], '**Use the relevant age and resistance-aware regimen.**',['Consider adjunctive dexamethasone with the first antibiotic dose when indicated.','Manage airway, seizures and shock in parallel.'],[['Prompt safe LP','Obtain before antibiotics if feasible'],['LP delayed','Do not delay empirical treatment'],['Parallel care','Resuscitation and complications']],[ME],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with suspected meningitis has a new focal neurological deficit and markedly impaired consciousness. Which LP principle is best?', 'Assess for reasons to defer LP and obtain indicated cranial imaging, while starting urgent treatment',[
 ('Perform LP immediately regardless of herniation risk','Certain findings require safety assessment.'),('A normal CT guarantees LP safety in every circumstance','Clinical contraindications and physiology still matter.'),('Delay antibiotics until every imaging and LP step is complete','This creates avoidable treatment delay.')],
 'LP safety and treatment urgency are separate decisions', '**Focal deficits and severely impaired consciousness can warrant imaging before LP.**',['Hemodynamic or respiratory instability also affects timing.','A scan does not replace full clinical safety assessment.'], '**Collect blood cultures and treat promptly if LP is deferred.**',['Use imaging to assess mass effect and other complications.','Revisit CSF sampling when appropriate.'],[['Safety','Clinical and imaging assessment'],['Treatment','Proceed urgently'],['Later','LP when suitable']],[ME],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which CSF pattern most strongly supports acute pyogenic bacterial meningitis?', 'Neutrophilic pleocytosis with low CSF-to-blood glucose and increased protein',[
 ('A pattern that excludes bacterial disease whenever lymphocytes are present','Cell patterns can vary with timing and prior treatment.'),('Normal protein and glucose as obligatory findings','They are not the usual pyogenic pattern.'),('A normal cell count as a universal exclusion of early disease','Rare early or immunocompromised presentations can be atypical.')],
 'The CSF profile supports probability but is not an absolute rule', '**Interpret glucose relative to a suitable blood glucose measurement.**',['Very early disease and prior antibiotics can modify findings.','TB and fungal meningitis can also lower glucose.'], '**Use Gram stain, culture and molecular tests alongside the profile.**',['A negative stain does not exclude infection.','Do not delay treatment in a high-risk syndrome because one parameter is atypical.'],[['Cells','Often neutrophilic'],['Glucose','Often low relative to blood'],['Protein','Usually increased']],[ME])
q('A 72-year-old with suspected bacterial meningitis receives ceftriaxone-based empirical therapy. Which additional coverage is important?', 'Ampicillin or amoxicillin for Listeria risk, with other additions guided by local pneumococcal resistance',[
 ('Ceftriaxone alone reliably covers Listeria','Listeria is not adequately covered by cephalosporins.'),('Metronidazole as the specific Listeria drug','It does not provide the required coverage.'),('A macrolide instead of the meningitis regimen','This is not an adequate standard empirical replacement.')],
 'Age and immune state alter the expected pathogen spectrum', '**WHO 2025 recommends added ampicillin/amoxicillin for relevant Listeria risk.**',['Older age, pregnancy and immunocompromise are important contexts.','Local resistance can require additional coverage for pneumococcus.'], '**Use meningitis doses and a complete empirical regimen.**',['Narrow when the organism and susceptibility are established.','Review allergy and renal function without compromising early treatment.'],[['Cephalosporin','Usual acute bacterial coverage'],['Listeria risk','Add appropriate aminopenicillin'],['Resistance','Local pneumococcal context']],[ME],style='clinical_scenario')
q('When should adjunctive dexamethasone be initiated when indicated for suspected acute bacterial meningitis in a suitable non-epidemic setting?', 'With or just before the first antibiotic dose',[
 ('Only after a week of completed antibiotics','That is not the evidence-based initial timing.'),('Instead of the first antibiotic dose','Steroids are adjunctive.'),('Only after every culture becomes negative','This misses the intended inflammatory phase.')],
 'Timing matters when modifying antibiotic-triggered meningeal inflammation', '**WHO supports early adjunctive IV corticosteroids in the appropriate pathway.**',['Continuation depends on the diagnostic and organism context.','Epidemic and unusual immune settings need specific guidance.'], '**Do not delay antibiotics to obtain a steroid dose.**',['Monitor glucose and other adverse effects.','Review ongoing need when CSF and microbiology clarify the cause.'],[['Timing','With first antibiotics'],['Role','Adjunctive'],['Continuation','Cause- and pathway-dependent']],[ME])
q('A patient received antibiotics before LP; CSF culture is negative but the clinical and CSF syndrome remains compatible with bacterial meningitis. What is the best interpretation?', 'Prior therapy can reduce culture yield, so integrate molecular tests, blood cultures and the full syndrome',[
 ('A negative culture always excludes bacterial meningitis','Pretreatment can sterilize cultures.'),('The CSF profile becomes entirely uninterpretable after any antibiotic dose','Some diagnostic information remains useful.'),('All treatment must be stopped immediately solely because culture is negative','The updated clinical probability should guide care.')],
 'A negative test after treatment is not the same as an untreated negative test', '**Antibiotic exposure affects organism recovery.**',['Molecular testing can remain informative.','Clinical response and alternative diagnoses also matter.'], '**Reassess the regimen and duration through the meningitis pathway.**',['Do not continue indefinitely without reviewing the evidence either.','Seek specialist input for unresolved or atypical disease.'],[['Culture','Yield can fall after therapy'],['Other tests','Blood cultures and molecular studies'],['Decision','Integrated probability and clinical course']],[ME],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A meningitis isolate is pneumococcus with relevant cephalosporin resistance. Which principle is appropriate?', 'Use susceptibility-directed CNS-active therapy, often requiring vancomycin with an appropriate third-generation cephalosporin pending expert review',[
 ('Use an oral low-dose penicillin automatically','Adequate CNS exposure and susceptibility are essential.'),('Ignore resistance because all beta-lactams penetrate inflamed meninges equally','Penetration does not overcome every MIC.'),('Stop treatment once the Gram stain identifies pneumococcus','Identification must be followed by effective definitive therapy.')],
 'The site-specific susceptibility interpretation matters', '**Meningitis susceptibility thresholds can differ from nonmeningeal infection.**',['Use an effective dose and monitor necessary drug exposure.','Local resistance informs initial empirical coverage.'], '**Narrow or modify from reliable laboratory results.**',['Discuss difficult MICs with microbiology and infectious disease.','Monitor clinical and neurological response.'],[['Organism','Pneumococcus'],['Site','CNS-specific susceptibility context'],['Treatment','Active CNS regimen and expert review']],[ME])
q('Which follow-up is particularly important after bacterial meningitis even when the acute infection resolves?', 'Assessment for hearing loss and other neurological, cognitive or functional sequelae',[
 ('No follow-up because negative cultures guarantee full recovery','Sequelae can persist after microbial cure.'),('Only a routine blood count without functional assessment','This misses important complications.'),('Automatic lifelong antibiotics for every survivor','That is not the recovery strategy.')],
 'Survival and recovery are different outcomes', '**Hearing impairment can follow bacterial meningitis.**',['Cognitive, neurological and psychosocial problems can also occur.','Early identification supports rehabilitation.'], '**Arrange appropriate audiology and clinical follow-up.**',['Explain symptoms requiring urgent reassessment.','Include family and rehabilitation services when needed.'],[['Hearing','Formal assessment when indicated'],['Neurology','Persistent deficits and seizures'],['Recovery','Rehabilitation and support']],[ME])
q('An acutely febrile patient has purpura, hypotension and altered consciousness. Which action is most appropriate for suspected invasive meningococcal disease?', 'Immediate resuscitation, effective antibiotics and appropriate infection-control/public-health action',[
 ('Wait for neck stiffness before treating','Meningococcemia can occur without classic meningitis signs.'),('Delay treatment for skin-biopsy confirmation','The syndrome can progress rapidly.'),('Treat the rash as diagnostic of a harmless viral illness','Purpura with shock is a high-risk presentation.')],
 'Meningococcal bloodstream disease can be fulminant', '**Shock, DIC and organ injury may progress quickly.**',['Blood cultures and molecular testing support confirmation.','Sampling must not delay emergency therapy.'], '**Manage the patient and exposure risk together.**',['Use the appropriate respiratory precautions.','Notify relevant public-health services to assess close contacts.'],[['Threat','Fulminant sepsis'],['Treatment','Immediate antibiotics and support'],['Public health','Contact assessment and prevention']],[ME],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Who particularly needs assessment for meningococcal antibiotic post-exposure prophylaxis?', 'Close contacts with relevant household or direct respiratory/oral secretion exposure',[
 ('Every person who merely passed the patient in a corridor','Casual proximity alone is not the usual criterion.'),('Only unvaccinated contacts, regardless of exposure','Vaccination does not automatically remove the prophylaxis indication.'),('No contact once the index patient has received one antibiotic dose','Exposed contacts can still require prevention.')],
 'Exposure intensity determines contact-management decisions', '**Household contacts and selected direct secretion exposures are important.**',['Public-health guidance defines the eligible window and contacts.','The preventive agent depends on susceptibility, pregnancy and local recommendations.'], '**Act promptly through the contact pathway.**',['Do not use vaccination as a substitute for indicated chemoprophylaxis.','Counsel contacts about warning symptoms.'],[['Qualifying exposure','Close household or secretion contact'],['Action','Prompt prophylaxis assessment'],['Counseling','Symptoms and vaccination review']],[ME])
q('A patient has fever, behavioral change, focal seizures and temporal-lobe MRI abnormalities. Which treatment should begin promptly while HSV encephalitis is investigated?', 'Intravenous acyclovir with appropriate renal dosing and monitoring',[
 ('Wait for the CSF PCR result in every case','Delay can worsen neurological outcome.'),('Oral low-dose acyclovir as adequate initial severe-encephalitis treatment','CNS infection requires an appropriate IV regimen.'),('Steroids alone because temporal lesions prove autoimmune disease','HSV is a treatable emergency and must be addressed.')],
 'Treatable encephalitis requires early empirical coverage', '**HSV can cause temporal-predominant necrotizing encephalitis.**',['CSF PCR and MRI support diagnosis.','Autoimmune encephalitis and other infections remain differentials.'], '**Monitor renal function and ensure appropriate hydration.**',['Acyclovir can cause renal and neurological toxicity with accumulation.','Treat seizures and assess airway or intensive-care needs.'],[['Syndrome','Encephalitis with temporal involvement'],['Immediate therapy','IV acyclovir'],['Safety','Renal adjustment and monitoring']],[EN,EI],style='clinical_scenario')
q('The first CSF HSV PCR is negative very early in a strongly compatible encephalitis syndrome. What is the best next principle?', 'Continue appropriate empirical acyclovir and repeat CSF PCR after an appropriate interval while reassessing alternatives',[
 ('Stop therapy permanently because the first result is always definitive','Early false negatives can occur.'),('Diagnose autoimmune encephalitis solely from the negative result','A negative early infectious test does not establish autoimmunity.'),('Use serum HSV IgG as definitive evidence of active CNS disease','Prior exposure is common and does not prove encephalitis.')],
 'Diagnostic timing can limit early sensitivity', '**A compatible syndrome can precede a positive CSF PCR.**',['Older IDSA guidance describes repeat testing in 3–7 days when appropriate.','Current neurological-infection guidance supports reassessment rather than automatic exclusion.'], '**Use clinical evolution, MRI and repeat testing together.**',['Discuss stopping criteria with the specialist team.','Investigate other infectious and immune causes when the picture remains uncertain.'],[['Early negative','Not universal exclusion'],['Immediate care','Continue justified treatment'],['Reassessment','Repeat CSF and broader differential']],[EN,EI])
q('A patient with fever and focal deficit has a ring-enhancing lesion with central diffusion restriction on MRI. What is a leading consideration?', 'Pyogenic brain abscess',[
 ('A finding that uniquely proves metastatic cancer','Tumors can mimic ring enhancement but central restriction supports abscess in context.'),('Uncomplicated meningitis without focal pathology','The lesion requires a focal differential.'),('A normal variant requiring no evaluation','This is an important pathological finding.')],
 'Diffusion imaging helps characterize a focal intracranial lesion', '**Pus can produce restricted diffusion.**',['The finding is strongly supportive but not perfectly specific.','Source assessment includes dental, ENT, pulmonary and hematogenous routes.'], '**Obtain neurosurgical and infectious-disease evaluation.**',['Aspiration can provide source control and microbiological diagnosis.','Avoid routine LP in a mass-lesion setting with potential pressure risk.'],[['MRI','Contrast plus DWI/ADC'],['Diagnostic target','Abscess versus other mass lesion'],['Next','Specialist sampling and treatment plan']],[BA],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which management principle is central to a typical community-acquired bacterial brain abscess in an immunocompetent adult?', 'Neurosurgical assessment for aspiration/excision plus a suitable CNS-active antimicrobial regimen',[
 ('A brief oral antibiotic course without assessing the lesion','This may be inadequate.'),('Routine LP as the best method to drain the abscess','LP does not drain a focal abscess and can be unsafe.'),('Steroids alone to suppress enhancement','They do not eradicate the infection.')],
 'A brain abscess combines a focal source problem and a prolonged antimicrobial need', '**A third-generation cephalosporin plus metronidazole is a common empirical community-acquired approach.**',['Trauma, neurosurgery and immunocompromise change the expected spectrum.','Aspirate microbiology permits targeted therapy.'], '**Individualize timing of sampling and antimicrobials to severity.**',['Do not delay treatment in sepsis or impending neurological catastrophe.','Follow clinical and serial imaging response.'],[['Source','Neurosurgical evaluation'],['Drug plan','CNS-active and source-specific'],['Follow-up','Clinical and imaging response']],[BA])
q('A patient develops trismus and stimulus-triggered painful spasms after a contaminated wound. Which mechanism causes tetanus?', 'Tetanospasmin-mediated inhibition of inhibitory neurotransmitter release',[
 ('Direct activation of the acetylcholine receptor as in an agonist overdose','The principal action is disinhibition through toxin effects on inhibitory neurons.'),('Destruction of the neuromuscular junction by an autoantibody','That is not the tetanus mechanism.'),('Primary blockade of motor acetylcholine release producing flaccid paralysis','That is more characteristic of botulinum toxin.')],
 'Loss of inhibition produces rigidity and spasms', '**Tetanus is a clinical diagnosis requiring urgent treatment.**',['Airway compromise and autonomic instability can be severe.','A small or healed wound does not exclude it.'], '**Provide toxin neutralization, wound care and intensive supportive management.**',['Control spasms and autonomic complications.','Use appropriate antibiotics and begin or complete active immunization.'],[['Tetanus','Disinhibition and spasticity'],['Botulism','Impaired motor transmitter release and flaccidity'],['Priority','Airway and spasm/autonomic control']],[TE],style='clinical_scenario')
q('What is the principal role of tetanus immune globulin in established tetanus?', 'Neutralizing unbound toxin; it does not reverse toxin already bound to nerve endings',[
 ('Immediately reversing all established spasms by removing bound toxin','Recovery requires restoration of neuronal function over time.'),('Replacing the need for wound management','The source of further toxin still needs attention.'),('Providing reliable lifelong active immunity after one dose','Passive protection does not replace vaccination.')],
 'Passive and active immunity have different purposes', '**TIG neutralizes toxin that has not yet bound.**',['Wound debridement and appropriate antibiotics reduce further toxin production.','Supportive care remains essential while established effects resolve.'], '**Vaccinate despite recovery from the disease.**',['Tetanus illness does not reliably confer protective immunity.','Coordinate the immunization schedule and future wound-prevention advice.'],[['TIG','Unbound toxin neutralization'],['Supportive care','Manages established effects'],['Vaccine','Builds future active protection']],[TE])

